<div>
<h2 id="harry-potter-kobo-plus">Harry Potter Arrives on Kobo Plus</h2>

<p>On July 24, 2026, Rakuten Kobo announced that the <strong>complete Harry Potter series is now available on Kobo Plus</strong> — including all seven ebooks and official audiobooks. For the first time, Kobo Plus subscribers can read the entire Wizarding World catalogue as part of their monthly membership.</p>

<p>This is a landmark addition to Kobo's growing subscription library. Harry Potter is one of the best-selling book series in history, with over 500 million copies sold worldwide. Having the complete series available on Kobo Plus — in EPUB format, DRM-protected but fully readable on any EPUB-compatible device — signals Kobo's ambition to compete directly with Kindle Unlimited and Audible in the subscription ebook and audiobook market.</p>

<p>This guide covers what Kobo Plus offers, how the Harry Potter books fit into the subscription, how to read the EPUB files on Kobo devices or convert them for other readers, and whether the subscription is worth your money.</p>

<h2 id="kobo-plus-pricing">Kobo Plus Subscription Tiers</h2>

<p>Kobo Plus offers three tiers, and the Harry Potter books are included in all of them:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Tier</th>
      <th>Monthly Price</th>
      <th>Includes</th>
      <th>Best For</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Kobo Plus Read</strong></td>
      <td>$9.99 / month</td>
      <td>Unlimited ebooks (EPUB), including Harry Potter</td>
      <td>eBook readers who prefer text-only reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Kobo Plus Listen</strong></td>
      <td>$9.99 / month</td>
      <td>Unlimited audiobooks, including Harry Potter narrated by Stephen Fry or Jim Dale</td>
      <td>Audiobook listeners on the go</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Kobo Plus Read & Listen</strong></td>
      <td>$12.99 / month</td>
      <td>Unlimited ebooks + audiobooks combined</td>
      <td>Readers who switch between reading and listening</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The bundled Read & Listen tier ($12.99/month) is the best value if you plan to work through all seven Harry Potter books. You can read an EPUB chapter on your Kobo e-reader during the commute, then pick up exactly where you left off via the audiobook while walking. Kobo's Whispersync-style technology syncs your position across ebooks and audiobooks within the Kobo ecosystem.</p>

<h2 id="which-harry-potter-books">Which Books Are Included?</h2>

<p>The entire seven-book series is available on Kobo Plus:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone</strong> (1997)</li>
  <li><strong>Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets</strong> (1998)</li>
  <li><strong>Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban</strong> (1999)</li>
  <li><strong>Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire</strong> (2000)</li>
  <li><strong>Harry Potter and the Order of the Phoenix</strong> (2003)</li>
  <li><strong>Harry Potter and the Half-Blood Prince</strong> (2005)</li>
  <li><strong>Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows</strong> (2007)</li>
</ol>

<p>Each book is available in the standard EPUB format with the original cover art, proper table of contents, and embedded typography. The audiobooks are available in both the Stephen Fry (UK) and Jim Dale (US) narrations, depending on your region.</p>

<p>Related titles from the Wizarding World — such as <em>Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them</em>, <em>Quidditch Through the Ages</em>, and <em>The Tales of Beedle the Bard</em> — are also included in the Kobo Plus catalogue as standalone ebooks.</p>

<h2 id="how-to-read-on-kobo">How to Read Harry Potter EPUBs on a Kobo E-Reader</h2>

<p>Reading the Harry Potter series on a Kobo device is the most straightforward option. Kobo e-readers are built around the EPUB format — they support EPUB 3 standards natively, including reflowable text, embedded fonts, and adjustable typesetting.</p>

<p>Here is how to get started:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Sign up for Kobo Plus</strong> at <a href="https://www.kobo.com/koboplus" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kobo.com/koboplus</a> — choose the Read tier ($9.99/mo) or the bundled Read & Listen tier ($12.99/mo). A 30-day free trial is available for new subscribers.</li>
  <li><strong>Browse the Harry Potter series</strong> — search for "Harry Potter" in the Kobo store or follow the curated collection link in the Kobo Plus section. All seven books display a "Read for Free" or "Listen for Free" badge.</li>
  <li><strong>Add to your library</strong> — tap "Add to My Library" or "Borrow" on each title. The book appears in your Kobo library immediately.</li>
  <li><strong>Sync your Kobo e-reader</strong> — connect your Kobo device to Wi-Fi, go to My Books, and pull down to sync. The Harry Potter books download as EPUB files to your device storage.</li>
</ol>

<p>Kobo e-readers give you excellent reading customization for the Harry Potter series. You can adjust font size, line spacing, margins, and the font face itself. The Kobo Libra Colour, for example, displays the chapter illustrations in a subtle gray-scale that retains the original art's detail. If you own a Kobo Clara BW or Kobo Sage, the reading experience is equally polished — EPUB rendering on Kobo is widely considered superior to Kindle's AZW rendering for typographically complex books.</p>

<p>For a comparison of Kobo models and how they handle EPUB files, see our <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">Kobo vs Kindle vs PocketBook 2026 guide</a>.</p>

<h2 id="read-on-other-devices">How to Read Harry Potter EPUBs on Non-Kobo Devices</h2>

<p>Kobo Plus ebooks use Adobe DRM (Digital Rights Management), which means the EPUB files are encrypted and tied to your Kobo account. However, there are legitimate ways to read the Harry Potter EPUBs on devices and apps that are not made by Kobo.</p>

<h3>On iPhone, iPad, or Android</h3>

<p>Kobo's mobile apps (available on iOS and Android) let you read your entire Kobo Plus library — including Harry Potter — directly on your phone or tablet. The apps sync your reading position with your Kobo e-reader, so you can switch between devices seamlessly. This is the easiest way to read on a non-Kobo device because it does not require any file handling: just install the Kobo app, sign in, and read.</p>

<h3>On Kindle Devices (via Calibre Conversion)</h3>

<p>Kindle devices do not natively support Adobe DRM-protected EPUB files from Kobo Plus. However, if you have purchased the Harry Potter ebooks outright (rather than borrowing them through the subscription), you can use Calibre to convert them for Kindle reading.</p>

<p>Our <a href="/blog/calibre-epub-to-kindle-workflow-2026/">Calibre EPUB to Kindle Workflow guide</a> covers the full process. In summary:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Download the purchased EPUB file from your Kobo library (via the Kobo desktop app)</li>
  <li>Use Calibre to convert EPUB to the AZW3 or KFX format that Kindle prefers</li>
  <li>Transfer to your Kindle via USB cable or Calibre's email-to-Kindle feature</li>
</ol>

<p><strong>Important note:</strong> Kobo Plus is a subscription rental, not a purchase. The EPUB files borrowed through Kobo Plus are DRM-protected and tied to the subscription. If you cancel your Kobo Plus subscription, you lose access to the borrowed Harry Potter books. Only ebooks you buy separately (outside the subscription) can be permanently downloaded and converted.</p>

<h3>On a Computer or Tablet EPUB Reader</h3>

<p>If you prefer reading on a larger screen, Kobo's web reader works in any modern browser. Books borrowed through Kobo Plus can be read directly at <a href="https://www.kobo.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kobo.com</a> after signing in. The web reader supports bookmarks, highlighting, and dictionary lookup.</p>

<p>For offline reading on a computer, use <strong>Adobe Digital Editions</strong> (free) to authorize and read Kobo EPUB files. Alternatively, <strong>Calibre</strong> has a built-in EPUB viewer that handles Adobe DRM content after authorization. See our <a href="/blog/best-epub-reader-apps-2026/">best EPUB reader apps guide</a> for a full comparison of reading software.</p>

<h2 id="kobo-plus-vs-kindle-unlimited">Kobo Plus vs Kindle Unlimited: EPUB vs Proprietary</h2>

<p>The Harry Potter addition puts Kobo Plus in a stronger competitive position against Amazon's Kindle Unlimited. Here is how the two subscription services compare:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th><strong>Kobo Plus</strong></th>
      <th><strong>Kindle Unlimited</strong></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Monthly price</td>
      <td>$9.99 (read or listen) / $12.99 (both)</td>
      <td>$11.99 (read only)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Ebook format</td>
      <td>EPUB 3 (open standard, reflowable)</td>
      <td>AZW / KFX (Kindle proprietary)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Audiobooks included</td>
      <td>Yes (Read & Listen tier, $12.99)</td>
      <td>No (Audible subscription separate)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Major exclusive series</td>
      <td>Harry Potter (+1.5 million other titles)</td>
      <td>Self-published exclusives, some trad-pub deals</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Device compatibility</td>
      <td>Kobo e-readers, iOS/Android apps, any EPUB reader</td>
      <td>Kindle e-readers, Fire tablets, Kindle apps</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Free trial</td>
      <td>30 days</td>
      <td>30 days</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The biggest practical difference is the <strong>EPUB format</strong>. Kobo Plus books are EPUB files — an open standard maintained by the W3C. You can read them on Kobo e-readers, any phone or tablet via the Kobo app, or on a computer. Kindle Unlimited books use Amazon's proprietary KFX format and can only be read on Kindle devices or through Kindle apps. If you value format flexibility and the ability to read on non-Amazon hardware, Kobo Plus has a clear advantage.</p>

<p>The Harry Potter deal is especially significant because it proves Kobo Plus can attract major traditionally-published titles. Kindle Unlimited has historically relied on a large catalogue of self-published and indie authors; Kobo Plus is now positioning itself as the subscription service that combines indie selection with blockbuster franchises.</p>

<h2 id="set-up-epub-library">Build Your Kobo Plus EPUB Library</h2>

<p>Once you have started reading the Harry Potter series on Kobo Plus, you may want to expand your EPUB library with complementary titles. Kobo Plus offers over 1.5 million ebooks and audiobooks, spanning fiction, non-fiction, and genre categories.</p>

<p>Here are strategies for getting the most out of your subscription:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Follow curated collections</strong> — Kobo Plus features seasonal and themed collections. July 2026 collections include "Summer Fantasy Reads," "Classics You Missed," and "New in Kobo Plus" which rotates weekly.</li>
  <li><strong>Use the Kobo app's discovery features</strong> — The Kobo mobile app (iOS/Android) has personalized recommendations based on what you borrow and read. After adding Harry Potter, watch for suggestions in the fantasy and young adult categories.</li>
  <li><strong>Sync between devices</strong> — Start reading on your Kobo e-reader, continue on your phone during lunch, and switch back in the evening. Kobo's syncing is reliable as long as your devices are connected to Wi-Fi.</li>
  <li><strong>Backup your highlights and notes</strong> — Kobo lets you export annotations via the web interface. For a more permanent record, use <a href="/blog/calibre-9-11-annotations-html-export-2026/">Calibre 9.11's HTML annotation export</a> to save your reading notes as an HTML file you can search later.</li>
</ul>

<p>For readers who want to maintain a permanent digital library outside the subscription, see our <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-Free EPUB Library guide</a> for strategies on buying and organizing ebooks you truly own.</p>

